<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antigonus I Monophthalmus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">382–<a href="/years/301-bce">301 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Antigonus · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antigonus I Monophthalmus, the 'One-Eyed', was a Macedonian general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> who became one of the most powerful successors after Alexander's death. He sought to reunite the empire and expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> for a time. His defeat and death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ipsus">Battle of Ipsus</a> in 301 BCE helped secure the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antigonus was born in Macedonia around 382 BCE to a nobleman named Philip. Ancient sources disagree about his family, some calling it peasant stock and others linking it to the royal house, but the family was in all probability socially prominent. When he first appears in the sources he commands a large part of Alexander's army, including about 7,000 allied Greek infantry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> records that an Antigonus lost an eye to a catapult bolt at the Siege of Perinthus in 340 BCE. He was an exceptionally large man, made more intimidating by the missing eye.</p><p>In 334 BCE he commanded the allied Greek infantry in Alexander's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and when Alexander marched east he appointed him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>. He took a garrison of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> into his own service and sent 1,500 of his mercenaries to reinforce Alexander before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Issus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Alexander's death in 323 BCE the Partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> confirmed Antigonus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, Lycaonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a>, Lycia and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisidia">Pisidia</a>. He incurred the enmity of the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a> by refusing to help Eumenes take Cappadocia, and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> with his son Demetrius. After Perdiccas was murdered in 321 BCE, Antigonus became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">Strategos</a> of Asia at Triparadisus and defeated the remnants of the Perdiccan faction at Orkynia and Cretopolis. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a>'s death in 319 BCE he defeated Eumenes, entered Babylon and seized the treasury at Susa. Seleucus fled to Ptolemy, and Ptolemy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> joined a league against Antigonus.</p><p>In the Third Diadochi War he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> after a year's siege. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, and a peace followed in 309 BCE in which Antigonus held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, the height of his power. After his son Demetrius defeated Ptolemy at sea at Salamis in 306 BCE and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, Antigonus took the title of king, which the other dynasts soon did as well. His invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> failed, and Demetrius's siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 305 and 304 BCE ended in a treaty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a>, Ptolemy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> allied against Antigonus, and the forces of Seleucus, Lysimachus and Cassander's general Prepelaos defeated him and Demetrius at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ipsus">Battle of Ipsus</a> in 301 BCE. He died there, struck by a javelin, in his eighty-first year; he had never lost a battle before. With his death plans to reunite Alexander's empire ended, and his kingdom was divided mostly between Lysimachus and Seleucus. Demetrius took control of Macedonia in 294 BCE, and Antigonus's descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid dynasty</a>, held it until the Roman conquest after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pydna">Battle of Pydna</a> in 168 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antigonus I Monophthalmus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Antigonus I Monophthalmus</b>, ‘the One-Eyed’, one of Alexander the Great's senior officers, had governed Phrygia since 333 BCE, and in the wars of the Successors after 323 BCE he built up the largest of the competing realms, stretching from western Asia Minor to Syria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 302 BCE, fearing <b>Antigonus I Monophthalmus</b>, who had put a kinsman to death, he fled east with a few followers to the stronghold of Kimiata in Paphlagonia and built up a following among the peoples of north-eastern Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Hellenistic kings Miletus was usually a nominally free city that passed between <b>Antigonus</b>, Lysimachus, the Ptolemies and the Seleucids; its citizens honoured Antiochus II with the title Theos, god, for removing the tyrant Timarchus around 259 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander the Great took Thracian contingents to Asia, and after his death in 323 BCE Seuthes III fought an indecisive battle against Lysimachus, the new Macedonian governor of Thrace, and later sided with <b>Antigonus</b> against him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…royal hypaspists under Alexander and the Companion cavalry under the regent Perdiccas, was appointed satrap of Babylonia in 320 BCE but was driven out by the dominant Diadochos <b>Antigonus I Monophthalmus</b> in 316 BCE, and spent several years in exile at the court of Ptolemy I in Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They settled at Petra in the 4th century BCE, and Diodorus Siculus describes the expeditions <b>Antigonus</b> sent against them in 312 BCE, though his account implies no town yet existed there.</blockquote><a href="/work/petra">Petra · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Elimiotis, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Ipsos, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.28,21.99&amp;year=-330" aria-label="Elimiotis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5090 724 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Antigonus I Monophthalmus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5540 994h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5540 994h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.28,21.99&amp;year=-330">What was here in 330 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
